+## Sizing: uniform, curvature-adaptive, anisotropic {#rem_sizing}
+
+`params.sizing` chooses how the remesher judges an edge, once per run. The
+driver, the sweep order, the freeze discipline and both distributed strategies
+are the same for all three; what differs is four decision makers:
+
+| Decision | `UNIFORM` | `CURVATURE` (Dunyach et al. 2013) | `ANISOTROPIC` (Jiao et al. 2010) |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| split wanted | $\ell > \ell_{\max}$ | $\ell > \tfrac43\min(L(a),L(b))$ | absolute longness **or** relative longness (§5.1) |
+| collapse wanted | $\ell < \ell_{\min}$ | $\ell < \tfrac45\min(L(a),L(b))$ | small angle, relative shortness, small triangle, relative small triangle (§5.2) |
+| collapse stretch | new edge $> \ell_{\max}$ | new edge $> \tfrac43\min(L_m, L(w))$ | new edge $> L$ |
+| flip wanted | valence | valence | Delaunay in the quad's normalised space (§4.3) |
+| free-vertex target | area-weighted centroid | centroid weighted by area $/\bar L^2$ | metric-mapped centroid offsets (§4.2) |
+| new-vertex position | chord midpoint | chord midpoint | quadric-error point along the normal |
+
+Every judgement goes through `MFTLRemeshEdgeWantsSplit` and
+`MFTLRemeshEdgeWantsCollapse` — the operators' predicates, the collapse's
+stretch clause and the spanning driver's convergence certificate alike — so they
+cannot disagree about what "too long" means. The `UNIFORM` branches are the
+expressions the remesher had before sizing existed, and every uniform digest is
+unchanged. The topological operators themselves are untouched: a split
+interpolates the curvature target to its new vertex and a collapse to its
+survivor, one line each.
+
+`max_edge_length` is always the longest edge the mode can want —
+$\tfrac43 L_{\max}$ under curvature sizing, $L = 1.5\,l$ under anisotropic —
+because the frozen band ($3\,\ell_{\max}$) and the parameter check read it.
+`MFTLRemeshUseCurvatureSizing` and `MFTLRemeshUseAnisotropicSizing` set the mode
+and derive that envelope; the settings layer does the same from `remesh.sizing`.
+
+### Curvature-adaptive {#rem_sizing_curvature}
+
+The target of a vertex comes from its maximum absolute curvature $\kappa$ and the
+approximation tolerance $\varepsilon$ — the largest gap between an edge and the
+surface it cuts — through the circumcircle of an equilateral triangle:
+
+$$
+  L(v) = \sqrt{6\varepsilon/\kappa - 3\varepsilon^2}\ \in [L_{\min}, L_{\max}],
+  \qquad \kappa = H + \sqrt{\max(H^2 - K, 0)} .
+$$
+
+$H = \tfrac12\lVert\Delta\mathbf x\rVert$ and $K = (2\pi - \sum\theta)/A_\text{mixed}$
+are the paper's operators (Meyer et al. 2003: cotangent Laplacian, angle defect,
+mixed Voronoi area with the obtuse cases), computed by
+`MFTLComputeVertexCurvatures` in `src/geometry/`. `MFTLRemeshCurvatureSizing`
+evaluates the formula without dividing before the clamps: $\kappa \le 0$ is the
+coarsest target, $\kappa \ge 2/\varepsilon$ the finest, and no input gives NaN.
+An edge's target is the smaller of its ends'. A feature, corner or rim vertex has
+no curvature of its own — a crease is unbounded, a half fan is not a curvature —
+and takes the mean of its non-feature neighbours (paper, Sect. 3, item 1).
+
+**One smoothing pass over the one-ring, before the length is taken.** Not in the
+paper; in its reference implementation (pmp-library smooths the curvature tensor
+once), and it is load-bearing. The edge rule takes a MINIMUM of two noisy
+estimates, which is biased low, so without the pass a remeshed mesh reads about
+6 % more curvature than its input and every pass that recomputes the field
+refines further: five repeated serial remeshes of an ellipsoid went
+4578 → 5092 → 5040 → 4952 → 4958 triangles and were still operating
+(`docs/analysis.md` §14.1). With it they reach a fixed point — no operation
+from the sixth call on — within 4 % of the first.
+
+**The field is computed once per kernel call and then carried.** A split
+interpolates it to the new vertex, a collapse to the survivor, compaction and
+reordering permute it (`verts.sizing`, an optional array of the vertex pool's one
+block: zero bytes unless enabled). Recomputing after every operation (what the
+old mftl did) would feed the chord midpoints the operators create straight back
+into the curvature.
+
+**Smoothing** moves a free vertex to
+$\sum_t |t|\rho_t\mathbf b_t / \sum_t |t|\rho_t$ with the density
+$\rho_t = \bar L_t^{-2}$ ($\bar L_t$ the mean of the three corner targets). This is a deliberate deviation from the printed Eq. 6,
+which weights by $|t|\,L(\mathbf b_t)$: the Optimal Delaunay Triangulation the
+paper cites weights by a density that DECREASES with the target, and so does
+pmp-library. The printed weight pulls vertices towards the coarse side, against
+the sizing field; `test_the_smoother_moves_towards_the_fine_side` asserts the
+sign. A ridge or rim vertex follows the 1D form of the same rule along its curve
+(edge midpoints weighted by length over squared target), whose fixed point is
+$\ell_1/L_1 = \ell_2/L_2$ and which is the uniform rule when the targets agree.
+
+**Distributed.** The field is a one-ring quantity, exact on owned vertices (the
+halo is vertex-complete) and partial on ghosts. No admitted operation reads a
+ghost's value; the convergence certificate does, and `hidden_long` is exact only
+if every copy of a vertex holds the same target. So
+`MFTLRemeshEnsemblePrepareSizing` runs the curvature pass, gives the ghosts their
+owners' values (`MFTLRemeshSyncGhostSizing`, `remesh_comm.c`, the route of the
+ghost-id exchange including its global fallback), runs the neighbour pass, and
+exchanges again. A redistribution does not carry the field, so the spanning
+driver recomputes it after every migration; each recomputation on a part-remeshed
+mesh is another chance to split at a dip the band's hysteresis then keeps, and
+the distributed fixed point carries 1.3 / 3.9 / 6.0 / 4.0 % more triangles than
+the one-rank fixed point at np = 2 / 4 / 8 / 27 (`docs/analysis.md` §14.2).
+Carrying the field through the remesher's migrations would remove that and
+needs a payload in the redistribution's wire format; not done.
+
+**Run ONE iteration per placement under curvature sizing.** The spanning driver
+converges every placement fully, so a budget of 4 is four times the serial
+kernel work per remesh, each time with a recomputed field. A uniform mesh is
+near converged every step and does not care; a curvature-sized mesh tracks a
+curvature that moves, and every extra pass splits and collapses for real: the
+LeVeque period at np = 4 kept 0.717 of its volume with three iterations per
+placement and 0.881 with one (serial: 0.903), at 45 against 19 ms/step
+(`docs/analysis.md` §14.5).
+
+### Anisotropic {#rem_sizing_anisotropic}
+
+The metric comes from the vertex QUADRIC
+$\mathbf A_v = \sum_f A_f\hat{\mathbf n}_f\hat{\mathbf n}_f^\top$
+(`MFTLComputeVertexQuadrics`, `MFTLVertexQuadric`),
+not from a curvature estimate: with eigenvalues $\lambda_1\ge\lambda_2\ge\lambda_3$
+the first eigenvector approximates the normal, the others the principal
+directions, and $\lambda_2/\lambda_1 \approx \epsilon_1^2\kappa_1^2/3$ for an
+edge $\epsilon_1$ along the maximum-curvature direction. The metric is (Eq. 10)
+
+$$
+  \tilde{\mathbf M} = \operatorname{diag}\big(s(\lambda_2), s(\lambda_3)\big)\ \text{in the frame}\ (\mathbf e_2, \mathbf e_3),
+  \qquad s(\lambda) = \min\big(\max(\lambda/\lambda_1, \psi_l), \psi_u\big),
+$$
+
+so the aspect ratio is bounded by $\sqrt{\psi_u/\psi_l} = 3.74$ at the defaults
+and the absolute size stays the desired length $l$. It is a one-ring sum of
+CURRENT geometry, so nothing is stored: a flip or a collapse clause evaluates it
+from the fans when it needs it, and the smoother once per (Jacobi) sweep.
+`MFTLSymmetricEigen3` is deterministic and relative, so the mode is independent of
+the length unit to the bit, like the uniform one.
+
+- **Split** (§5.1): absolute longness — longer than $L$ and the longest edge of
+  its triangles — OR relative longness — longer than $l$, opposite an angle above
+  $\theta_l$, no edge of its triangles under $s$. Alternatives: the old mftl
+  returned at the first when an edge was long but not the longest, so the second
+  was never tried.
+- **Collapse** (§5.2): opposite an angle under $\theta_s$ in a triangle smaller
+  than $l$; under $r$ times the longest edge of its triangles; the shortest edge of
+  triangles all under $S = Rl$; or, in triangles under $l$, under $R$ times the
+  longest edge in physical AND normalised space. The merit clauses — link
+  condition, valence, stretch at $L$, fold — are the uniform ones.
+- **Flip** (§4.3): the four vertices' quadrics are summed and the quad mapped to
+  that sum's normalised space; flip when the angles opposite the edge sum to
+  more than $\pi$, tested as $\sin(\alpha+\beta) < -\tau$ ($\tau = 10^{-4}$,
+  because a flip changes the fans the metric is read from). Refused, as in the
+  paper, when a face normal would turn by more than 45° (the shared fold
+  threshold is about 78°), and — beyond the paper — when a new triangle would
+  face against the triangle across one of the quad's rim edges where the old
+  one did not: the Delaunay criterion flips far more often than the valence
+  one and reaches creases tighter than an edge (CLAUDE.md deferred issue 25).
+- **Smoothing** (§4.2): each incident face's centroid offset $\mathbf d$ is mapped
+  by that face's metric,
+  $\tilde{\mathbf d} = \mathbf T\sqrt{\mathbf M}\mathbf T^\top\mathbf d$, the face quadric being the corners' with weight 1 / $10^{-6}$ /
+  $10^{-12}$ for free / ridge / corner vertices. The paper sums the mapped offsets
+  under a relaxation factor "for avoiding mesh folding"; here they are averaged
+  with the face areas over $\sum|t|\sigma_2$, the larger scale, so no direction's
+  gain exceeds one and an isotropic metric gives exactly the uniform smoother's
+  centroid. (Over $\sum|t|\sqrt{\sigma_2\sigma_3}$ the gain across the curved
+  direction reached 1.93, and the LeVeque sphere lost 39 % of its volume.) As
+  the paper prescribes, a step that would turn an incident face over is halved,
+  and dropped after four halvings.
+- **Placement** (§5.1–5.2): a split's vertex and a collapse's merged vertex move
+  from the midpoint along the edge normal to the minimum of the quadric error of
+  both endpoints' faces, capped at a quarter of the edge; a split falls back to
+  the midpoint when the point would fold a new triangle (an offered long edge is
+  then still always split). It recovers part of the $h^2\kappa/8$ a midpoint cuts
+  off: a sphere refined from 42 vertices ends at mean $|1-r| = 0.042$ against
+  0.059. Behind `qem_placement`, on by default only in this mode.
+
+**Where this deviates from the paper, on purpose.**
+
+- **The sweep order is MFTL's.** It runs split, collapse, flip, smooth, not
+  smooth, contract, flip, split, flip, smooth, and sweeps in pool order rather
+  than sorted by length. The paper's order held the volume worse in this mode too
+  (+0.52 % against +0.33 % at refinement 4) and sorting changed nothing, so both
+  were removed (see "The sweep order" above).
+- **Ridge and rim vertices keep the uniform curve smoothing**, and the tangent
+  projection uses the vertex normal rather than the quadric's null space.
+
+**The anisotropy develops over passes**, as the paper says. A single pass on an
+isotropic mesh moves it part of the way: on an ellipsoid's waist the mean aspect
+ratio went to 1.60, against 1.38 for the uniform control, where the metric asks for
+about 2 (`test_anisotropic_mode_stretches_along_the_flat_direction`).
+
+**Over the whole LeVeque period it holds the volume best of the three modes**, to
++0.06 % at refinement 5, within the paper's 0.1 % (uniform loses 1.2 %). Two costs
+come with it (`docs/analysis.md` §14.4, §14.8):
+
+- **It keeps the triangles it grows**: about three times the initial count at t = T.
+- **It returns the roughest surface at the vertex scale.** Its vertices sit closest to
+  the exact surface, but the discrete curvature a front tracker would compute there is
+  the noisiest of the three modes.
+
+---
+
